<v Diane Wu>They're not yelling at this one.
<v Diane Wu>它们没对这个尖叫。

00:52:34.230 --> 00:52:35.240
<v John Zimmerman>Yeah. [LAUGHS]
<v John Zimmerman>是的。[笑]

00:52:35.240 --> 00:52:41.850
<v Diane Wu>Next, John got this special light off of Amazon, the kind that projects colored patterns onto your house at Christmas.
<v Diane Wu>接下来，约翰从亚马逊买了这个特殊的灯，那种圣诞节投射彩色图案到房子上的那种。

00:52:41.850 --> 00:52:47.990
<v John Zimmerman>It bothers them a little bit, but that's good. They need to get used to flashing lights from camera flashes and whatnot.
<v John Zimmerman>它让它们有点困扰，但这很好。它们需要习惯照相机的闪光灯等等。

00:52:47.990 --> 00:52:52.600
<v Diane Wu>Grant marches in with a kid-sized picnic chair and plops it down in the middle of the flock.
<v Diane Wu>格兰特带了一把儿童大小的野餐椅，放在了火鸡群中间。

00:52:52.600 --> 00:52:55.560
<v John Zimmerman>So another thing we'll do, we'll bring a chair in here and we'll just sit.
<v John Zimmerman>所以我们还要做的一件事是，我们带一把椅子进去，然后我们就坐下。

00:52:55.560 --> 00:53:11.370
<v Diane Wu>This is to get them used to being around people. John's also enlisted the neighbor kids to come by regularly to sit with the birds and pick them up a lot. At this point, a few weeks out from Thanksgiving, it all comes down to who's hot. The next culling will be for beauty.
<v Diane Wu>这是为了让它们习惯和人在一起。约翰还招募了邻居家的孩子定期来这里坐着，经常抱抱这些鸟。此时，离感恩节还有几周，最终取决于谁最火。下一次筛选将是为了美观。

00:53:11.370 --> 00:53:16.380
<v John Zimmerman>Obviously, here, we want them to have perfect feathers. So we're really watching their tail feathers and how they fan out.
<v John Zimmerman>显然，在这里，我们希望它们有完美的羽毛。所以我们真的在观察它们的尾羽以及它们是如何展开的。

00:53:16.380 --> 00:53:19.250
<v Diane Wu>That one looks really full. It's like a full circle.
<v Diane Wu>那一只看起来很丰满。就像一个完整的圆圈。

00:53:19.250 --> 00:53:22.250
<v John Zimmerman>I see three good choices right there. Three nice tails, yeah.
<v John Zimmerman>我看到了三个很好的选择。三个漂亮的尾巴，是的。

00:53:22.250 --> 00:53:23.160
<v Diane Wu>Right here in the front?
<v Diane Wu>就在前面吗？

00:53:23.160 --> 00:53:23.690
<v Grant>Oh, my.
<v Grant>噢，天哪。

00:53:23.690 --> 00:53:38.590
<v John Zimmerman>Yeah. [LAUGHS] My problem has been, talking with other chairmen who've gone through this process, they say, oh, you'll be able to pick the best two, no problem, that it will be very easy to pick the final two. Now, maybe in the next week or two, the cream will rise to the top, and we'll find the two that are the best.
<v John Zimmerman>是的。[笑] 我的问题是，和其他经历过这个过程的主席交谈，他们说，哦，你选出最好的两只会没问题，选出最后两只会很容易。现在，也许在接下来的一两周内，精华会脱颖而出，我们会找到最好的两只。

00:53:38.590 --> 00:54:00.040
<v Diane Wu>But none of them are jumping out to him yet. There's a test John's going to run on the birds soon. He and Grant have built a box to mimic the ones that the turkeys stand on at the South Lawn. And he plans to put each of these birds on the box and see who stays on it the longest. He wants them to hit at least a minute. They did a trial run back in September. He shows me the notes.
<v Diane Wu>但目前还没有哪只让他眼前一亮。约翰很快要对这些鸟进行测试。他和格兰特造了一个箱子来模仿火鸡站在南草坪上的那个。他计划把每只鸟放在箱子上，看谁站得最久。他希望它们至少能坚持一分钟。九月份他们做了一次试运行。他给我看笔记。

00:54:00.040 --> 00:54:04.900
<v John Zimmerman>This is the team test. So this is how many seconds they were standing on top of the box.
<v John Zimmerman>这是团队测试。这是它们站在箱子顶上的秒数。

00:54:04.900 --> 00:54:06.620
<v Diane Wu>And so their range is like--
<v Diane Wu>它们的范围大约是……

00:54:06.620 --> 00:54:08.810
<v John Zimmerman>7 seconds, but then there's a couple--
<v John Zimmerman>7 秒，但有几只……

00:54:08.810 --> 00:54:12.290
<v Grant>One minute and a half. Two, two minutes.
<v Grant>一分半。两分，两分钟。

00:54:12.290 --> 00:54:14.610
<v Diane Wu>Could you do a little demo of that?
<v Diane Wu>你能演示一下吗？

00:54:14.610 --> 00:54:15.410
<v John Zimmerman>I'll try.
<v John Zimmerman>我试试。

00:54:15.410 --> 00:54:16.090
<v Diane Wu>OK.
<v Diane Wu>好的。

00:54:16.090 --> 00:54:19.680
<v Ira Glass>John goes to get a turkey, and Grant explains how his dad picks it up.
<v Ira Glass>约翰去抓一只火鸡，格兰特解释他爸爸是怎么抱它的。

00:54:19.680 --> 00:54:25.650
<v Grant>So you grab the legs, put its belly on the ground, and then put your other hand under its belly.
<v Grant>你抓住腿，让它肚子贴地，然后把另一只手放在它肚子下面。

00:54:25.650 --> 00:54:26.510
<v Diane Wu>OK.
<v Diane Wu>好的。

00:54:26.510 --> 00:54:27.500
<v Grant>[CHUCKLES]
<v Grant>[轻笑]

00:54:27.500 --> 00:54:29.500
<v Grant>[TURKEYS GOBBLING]
<v Grant>[火鸡的咯咯声]

00:54:29.500 --> 00:54:31.490
<v Grant>[WINGS FLUTTERING]
<v Grant>[翅膀拍打声]

00:54:31.490 --> 00:54:34.940
<v Diane Wu>That one's flapping its wings a bunch. That did not want to get picked up.
<v Diane Wu>那只拍打了很多次翅膀。它根本不想被抱。

00:54:34.940 --> 00:54:38.850
<v John Zimmerman>He's getting a poor grade. But we'll give him a shot here.
<v John Zimmerman>它成绩很差。但我们还是让它试试。

00:54:38.850 --> 00:54:40.700
<v Diane Wu>OK, and you set it down.
<v Diane Wu>好的，然后你把它放下。

00:54:40.700 --> 00:54:43.020
<v John Zimmerman>And now the hope would be that he would just--
<v John Zimmerman>现在希望它能只是……

00:54:43.020 --> 00:54:46.630
<v Grant>Stand and do nothing. Just "Hi, people."
<v Grant>站着什么都不做。只是“嗨，大家好。”

00:54:46.630 --> 00:54:48.700
<v Grant>[TURKEYS GOBBLING]
<v Grant>[火鸡的咯咯声]

00:54:48.700 --> 00:54:49.890
<v Diane Wu>What's he doing?
<v Diane Wu>它在干嘛？

00:54:49.890 --> 00:55:00.390
<v John Zimmerman>He's behaving pretty well. I mean, he's not comfortable. It would be nice if he would just stand here and strut, but he's obviously a little concerned.
<v John Zimmerman>它表现还不错。我的意思是，它不舒服。如果它能只是站着炫耀一下会很好，但它显然有点担心。

00:55:00.390 --> 00:55:06.270
<v Diane Wu>We watched the bird hunch kind of nervously for 48 seconds. He doesn't strut.
<v Diane Wu>我们看着那只鸟紧张地弓着背 48 秒。它没有炫耀。

00:55:06.270 --> 00:55:07.920
<v John Zimmerman>The perfect bird would just want to stand here.
<v John Zimmerman>完美的鸟只会想站在那儿。

00:55:07.920 --> 00:55:27.570
<v Diane Wu>The whole time I'm talking to John, Grant is quietly running around us, inventing chores for himself. He brings in a pumpkin, feeds it to the turkeys, tries to turn on the Christmas lights, keeps picking up different birds, gets a random medal and puts it on one of them, climbs up onto the makeshift stage and squats down, posing as a turkey.
<v Diane Wu>我和约翰交谈时，格兰特一直安静地在我们周围跑来跑去，发明些杂活儿干。他拿来一个南瓜，喂给火鸡，试图打开圣诞灯，不断抱起不同的鸟，弄到一个随便的奖章并戴在其中一只身上，爬上临时舞台蹲下，像火鸡一样姿势。

00:55:27.570 --> 00:55:40.680
<v Diane Wu>I get the feeling that he really wants to show me all of this. He's excited to share his turkey project, but is too shy to say much or directly get my attention. John's going to be bringing Grant to the White House, too, later this month.
<v Diane Wu>我觉得他真的很想向我展示这一切。他很兴奋能分享他的火鸡项目，但太害羞而说不出什么，或直接引起我的注意。约翰这个月晚些时候也会带格兰特去白宫。

00:55:40.680 --> 00:56:02.610
<v John Zimmerman>My son is 9, going on 10, at a shy age. And I expect him to be standing up there with me in front of the cameras. And it is a little bit disconcerting or overwhelming to him. I don't think he's quite aware of what this experience is going to be. So we're training the turkeys, but we're also, in a way, educating and training my son to be part of this event.
<v John Zimmerman>我儿子 9 岁，快 10 岁了，正是害羞的年纪。我期待他和我一起站在摄像机前。这对他来说有点令人不安或不知所措。我不认为他完全意识到这次体验会是什么样子。所以我们正在训练火鸡，但某种程度上，我们也在教育和训练我儿子成为这个活动的一部分。

00:56:02.610 --> 00:56:09.610
<v Diane Wu>Part of that training is watching past pardoning ceremonies on YouTube together. Part of it is getting him to talk to strangers, like me.
<v Diane Wu>部分训练是和他一起在 YouTube 上观看过去的赦免仪式。部分是让他和陌生人交谈，比如我。

00:56:09.610 --> 00:56:17.220
<v John Zimmerman>Just getting him used to interacting with adults because he's a nine-year-old boy, and that's not their favorite thing to do always, so.
<v John Zimmerman>让他习惯和成年人交流，因为他是个九岁的男孩，这总不是他们最喜欢做的事，所以。

00:56:17.220 --> 00:56:23.260
<v Diane Wu>Have you been practicing at all, Grant, for the White House ceremony or thinking about how that'll go for you?
<v Diane Wu>格兰特，你为白宫仪式做过什么准备或者想过会怎么样吗？

00:56:23.260 --> 00:56:30.910
<v Grant>Mm, not really.
<v Grant>嗯，没有。

00:56:30.910 --> 00:56:33.410
<v Grant>I'm just shy.
<v Grant>我只是害羞。

00:56:33.410 --> 00:56:34.940
<v Diane Wu>Are turkeys shy, too?
<v Diane Wu>火鸡也害羞吗？

00:56:34.940 --> 00:56:55.340
<v Grant>Um, sort of. Depends on what you do to them. For the most part, I think they are not very shy. If you just stand there, they want to go see, who is this? Who is this? They're only shy if-- probably if you're trying to catch one.
<v Grant>嗯，有点。取决于你对它们做什么。在很大程度上，我认为它们不是很害羞。如果你只是站在那儿，它们想去看看，这是谁？这是谁？它们只有在……可能在你试图抓它们时才害羞。

00:56:55.340 --> 00:56:58.010
<v Diane Wu>But usually they're kind of curious and will eventually come up?
<v Diane Wu>但通常它们有点好奇，最终会过来？

00:56:58.010 --> 00:56:58.880
<v Grant>Yeah.
<v Grant>是的。

00:56:58.880 --> 00:57:00.360
<v Diane Wu>Are you like that, you think?
<v Diane Wu>你觉得你也是那样吗？

00:57:00.360 --> 00:57:01.460
<v Grant>Mm-hmm.
<v Grant>嗯哼。

00:57:01.460 --> 00:57:05.360
<v John Zimmerman>Yeah. It's all about getting acclimated.
<v John Zimmerman>是的。这全是为了适应。

00:57:05.360 --> 00:57:35.450
<v Diane Wu>John's hoping that at the big press conference, when it's time to announce the turkeys' names, Grant will say them. The week of Thanksgiving, when I watch all of this, that's the part I'm going to be leaning in for-- not to see whether or not the turkey flaps on the box, but Grant's moment to take the stage and strut, if he wants.
<v Diane Wu>约翰希望在大型新闻发布会上，宣布火鸡名字的时候，格兰特会说出名字。感恩节那周，当我看到这一切时，我会倾听那个部分——不是为了看火鸡是否在箱子上扑腾，而是格兰特登台并炫耀的时刻，如果他愿意的话。

00:57:35.450 --> 00:57:38.790
<v Ira Glass>Diane Wu is one of the producers of our show.
<v Ira Glass>黛安·吴是我们节目的制作人之一。

00:57:38.790 --> 00:58:08.940
<v Ira Glass>["WHALE" BY YELLOW OSTRICH]
<v Ira Glass>["WHALE" BY YELLOW OSTRICH]

